# ---------- check-typecheck-authority (2026-08-11) ----------
# REPLACES the check:tsc-error-count run that used to sit here.
#
# The type-check itself is NOT gone — it moved to CI. Why it had to move:
#
#   The line here used to claim "~3-15s depending on cache state". MEASURED
#   2026-08-11 at b7ecc0b5: 881s — 14.7 MINUTES — at 95-100% CPU, for the
#   ordinary passing case (229/229). That is 59-88x the advertised price, and
#   it fired on 21 of the last 40 commits (52.5%). The same understatement sat
#   in verify-core.yml ("~50s" vs a measured 481-640s).
#
#   Dropping --force does not rescue it. Measured back to back on the same tree:
#     tsc -b --noEmit --force  881.5s / 229 errors
#     tsc -b --noEmit          845.6s / 229 errors  (both .tsbuildinfo warm)
#   35.9s saved out of 881 — 4.1%. Neither tsconfig sets composite/incremental,
#   so `tsc -b` has no up-to-date check to short-circuit. There is no cheap
#   local answer to buy, so none was faked.
#
#   And the mispricing already cost a real incident. The MP-274 session budgeted
#   the documented ~15s, ended while the gate ground on, and left that wave
#   staged-but-uncommitted for over an hour WHILE its edge function was already
#   deployed to prod (v47). Prod ran code no commit described, and any worker
#   redeploying from source would have silently reverted Sam's phone push.
#
#   A 15-minute hook on half of all commits is not a guard — it is a standing
#   invitation to `git commit --no-verify`, which bypasses the ~40 OTHER checks
#   in this file that all cost under a second each. Keeping it here was costing
#   more safety than it bought.
#
# So: .github/workflows/verify-core.yml is now the sole authority. It runs the
# full --force rebuild on every push to main and every PR, from a cold
# actions/checkout where no stale cache can exist. Last 12 runs: 12/12 green.
# Cost of the trade: a type error surfaces ~10 min after push instead of before
# the commit. Run `npm run check:tsc-error-count` any time to check locally.
#
# What runs here instead asserts that CI authority still exists — triggers,
# verify:core wiring, and the --force flag, end to end. Sub-second, no compiler.
# Without it, narrowing verify-core.yml would silently leave the repo with NO
# type-check at all, and nothing would go red.
if match '^(\.github/workflows/verify-core\.yml$|package\.json$|scripts/check-tsc-error-count\.mjs$|scripts/check-typecheck-authority\.mjs$)'; then
  echo "[pre-commit] type-check authority chain touched — running check:typecheck-authority…"
  node scripts/check-typecheck-authority.mjs
fi

# ---------- check-landing-truth-floor (2026-06-19) ----------
# Trigger: any landing surface that reads landing_live_stats(), the public
# Landing page, or the guard script itself. Locks the `??` fallback ceiling
# per landing_live_stats() key so a future Lovable/AI completion can't re-
# introduce the 104/95/123 lie that clamped truth=41 UPWARD twice (feb05b97
# fix + 86f8d98e finish). Cost when fired: ~30ms (6 small files × 6 keys).
# MP-373: the trigger used to be a SECOND hand list, and it had drifted off the
# guard's own TRACKED_FILES -- it named src/pages/Landing.tsx (gone from the
# repo) and missed src/pages/Apply.tsx, the page that shipped the stale `?? 104`
# floor this guard exists to stop. The guard now publishes its list and this
# asks for it, so the two cannot come apart again. If that call fails for any
# reason we fall back to running the guard, never to skipping it.
